[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054625 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054542 :question/id #uuid "de20802c-6a7e-5d77-b2ce-26f98dc68b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4095" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the first term of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{3})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{8x}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4) = \\log\\left(2x\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd9426d-8ce3-564d-85c5-4ae82289325d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608381 :question-generator/id #uuid "4ceb511d-7e72-5ab3-b790-cf8a4b698bbb" :question-generator/script "generators/d2/d22bc61dbe5d5e954a0b7cce9d800e9795e2ef28137adc1a72a2db643e6feafd/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b56a6dea-6c8e-5fa5-9b5b-9c3a0d2ca34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log (x) + 3\\log (y) - \\log (z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051273} {:answer/id #uuid "be3f403a-db0e-5f33-8101-a0925b25258f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 