d "6929a7d0-50d3-5bd8-b005-5eaf6c8afc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542922} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2475f77-a3bc-521f-922f-608b63eb6022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34847574-efca-5670-8924-e70a3bcc31ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-66923" :question/problem "${log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)={log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z)={log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z) & ={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{1/2})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10851533-4282-5b80-bae7-98d8fc92685d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542678} {:answer/id #uuid "290b87bb-8570-5cb4-83b9-b6d2e59941cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542749} {:answer/id #uuid "599a54dc-6c33-56a6-8830-1ae45124f78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542884} {:answer/id #uuid "84635862-dc5f-5a84-88a5-ebc9a5b22bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542994} {:answer/id #uuid "91fec59e-a5fa-5664-9d8b-2381d008fe09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{log}_{3}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10851533-4282-5b80-bae7-98d8fc92685d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542678} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ccb3dbb-60a9-5fb3-a5b6-44c5f5ad437b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3b649a8-d282-53b7-b8c8-e883fd47c0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79406" :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z}).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11acc97c-dfd0-5a77-8983-31e3ebe5ece1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608347} {:answer/id #uuid "154d9344-bd5a-5e0c-a0b1-79e6281b401c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608348} {:answer/id #uuid "51ad0c7e-5d40-55ea-9122-d78869b00e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608353} {:answer/id #uuid "865f230e-90aa-5235-95bd-e89b98b7fac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608362} {:answer/id #uuid "a89da8ce-91e1-5bca-abdc-df5de9e24617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "154d9344-bd5a-5e0c-a0b1-79e6281b401c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608348} :answer-field/id #uuid "c107aa3c-cee0-501f-b460-3e984500c21d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8aed1f5f-94a6-53e0-bffe-9da4a87b67b6" :question/problem "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\n$\\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{4}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}z^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}z^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -\\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8fc261-31e0-541b-91c1-4411d4e0973a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{4}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{3}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608346} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2255ce-42f5-5639-ad7c-5934d1679230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{4}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608350} {:answer/id #uuid "b7743c63-7c8e-545b-bf44-6feca4620c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{x^{3}y^{2}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608369} {:answer/id #uuid "c875ea58-cd4e-59c0-88f0-b8c7c0acbd87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{4}\\sqrt{z}}{x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608377} {:answer/id #uuid "f68940be-ab71-51eb-891c-78779bd427d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y\\sqrt{z}}{x^{3}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d2255ce-42f5-5639-ad7c-5934d1679230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{4}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608350} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e73368-5863-5f7f-af94-141abd2cd7db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91336788-d4fe-55bb-b76d-d8f6b2ce687c" :question/problem "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\nExpress $3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)-4\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& 3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)-4\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we combine the factors with the same sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{4}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& 3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)-4\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{4}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a6bdfe3-95b1-5215-8aea-e1de730debb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)-\\log\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608352} {:answer/id #uuid "67be44d9-5f17-58e8-8e87-74b65873f37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\log\\left(z\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+3\\log\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608356} {:answer/id #uuid "6c78181a-ca0d-5827-a7de-19e230e2f386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+3\\log\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608357} {:answer/id #uuid "76a43129-546c-5f7d-b75e-548ae0305794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\log\\left(z\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+\\log\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608361} {:answer/id #uuid "c2561d01-4222-5545-af8f-bcd357b613a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+\\log\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c78181a-ca0d-5827-a7de-19e230e2f386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+3\\log\\left(y\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608357} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f2ce376-ed99-5857-b5a3-d1978349d5ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b34f9130-5590-5e81-9633-557267f7645f" :question/problem "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\n$\\log\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{5}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}z^{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}z^{5}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &= -5\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+3\\log\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= -5\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+3\\log\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ae42d0-af93-5a07-bc44-399238b45527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608351} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf8249c-6893-5289-9815-92dc50a80e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608354} {:answer/id #uuid "728705fc-d1f3-5228-b4b6-9e1bfa6ee590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{4}{x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608359} {:answer/id #uuid "7397b068-49be-5949-ae48-cbefa5902e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{4x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608360} {:answer/id #uuid "9a05a335-9f6e-5aff-bfbb-4286d2b8cc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40ae42d0-af93-5a07-bc44-399238b45527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608351} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e856a3-ef2f-56a4-b6fb-83cd50a4847a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "baf39665-b368-5de7-b858-419c7ef09632" :question/problem "Assume $x>0$.\n\nExpress $-3\\log_{3}\\left(2\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, rewrite the powered numerical term and evaluate the power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -3\\log_{3}\\left(2\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log_{3}\\left(2^{3}\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log_{3}\\left(8\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, combine the factors with the same sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -\\log_{3}\\left(8\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(8x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms and simplifying the numerical factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(8x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{4}{8x}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -3\\log_{3}\\left(2\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x>0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176ba7d7-7ae0-563a-b102-c5dade7628a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(x^{3}y^{4}\\sqrt{z}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608349} {:answer/id #uuid "abe850e5-e434-58c4-9fbd-a1769dd958ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(xy^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608367} {:answer/id #uuid "c74e7c8d-9ca0-5f45-897e-619bdb6e62c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608376} {:answer/id #uuid "ca36776c-361f-5eda-8e78-3d7efc9352e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}y\\sqrt{z}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608378} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d4d2b4-34a4-5c16-abad-9a2479672191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7d4d2b4-34a4-5c16-abad-9a2479672191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608380} :answer-field/id #uuid "71c5fe37-6faf-5baa-bee8-0b0352a85ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdffb806-9aac-5618-883f-6ba573ab4d59" :question/problem "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\nExpress $-2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we combine the factors with the same sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product, Quotient, and Power Rules of Logarithms"}]
